Successful Case 1: Nestlé's ERP Implementation

 b.1. Company Overview 
Nestlé implemented its enterprise system to streamline its global operations and improve overall efficiency. 
By integrating key business functions like supply chain management, finance, and human resources into a single system, 
the company was able to standardize processes across its diverse operations worldwide. This unified approach enabled better 
decision-making through real-time access to data, which is crucial for responding quickly to market shifts. The system helped 
reduce operational costs by automating manual tasks and optimizing resource management. It also ensured regulatory compliance 
across various regions and improved customer experiences through faster service and personalized offerings, helping Nestlé 
maintain its competitive edge in the global market.

b.2. Implementation Process
Nestlé’s enterprise system implementation followed a comprehensive process, starting with careful planning and selection of the right platform. 
The company sought a solution that could integrate its diverse operations globally, aligning finance, supply chain, procurement, and customer 
service functions. The implementation process involved detailed process mapping, system design, and testing to ensure the system met Nestlé’s 
unique business needs. The company also focused heavily on training employees and aligning their workflows to the new system. Nestlé adopted a 
phased rollout approach, starting with pilot programs in select regions and gradually expanding to its global operations. Despite the complexity,
Nestlé dedicated significant resources to ensure the system was implemented smoothly without major disruptions to day-to-day operations.

b.3. Key success factors
Several factors contributed to the success of Nestlé’s enterprise system implementation. One of the key success factors was strong leadership and 
clear communication across all levels of the organization, which helped manage the change effectively. Nestlé ensured that employees were properly
trained and had the necessary support to transition smoothly to the new system. Another critical factor was the careful selection of the right enterprise 
platform that could scale with the company’s global operations while integrating well with existing systems. Nestlé also maintained a focus on continuous 
improvement, constantly evaluating the system’s performance and making necessary adjustments. Collaboration with external consultants and technology 
partners who brought expertise to the implementation process further ensured that the system met the company’s objectives.

 b.4. outcomes & impact
The implementation of the enterprise system at Nestlé led to significant positive outcomes. The company gained greater operational efficiency, 
with improved data visibility and real-time insights into business performance, which facilitated better decision-making. The integration of 
various functions streamlined supply chain management, improved inventory control, and enhanced customer service. Financial processes became more 
standardized, reducing errors and improving compliance across regions. Additionally, the enterprise system enabled Nestlé to scale its operations 
more effectively as the company continued to grow globally. The long-term impact was improved profitability, reduced operational costs, and a stronger 
competitive position in the market. Ultimately, the successful implementation helped Nestlé achieve better coordination across its global network, 
making it more agile in responding to market demands.

Failure Case 2:  Hershey's ERP Implementation

 c.1. Company Overview 
Hershey implemented its enterprise system to enhance operational efficiency, improve customer service, and support its global expansion. 
With a large-scale production and distribution network, Hershey needed a unified system to integrate various business functions, such as 
manufacturing, inventory management, sales, and finance, ensuring smoother workflows across all departments. The enterprise system allowed 
the company to standardize processes and provide real-time data, enabling better decision-making and faster responses to market demands. 
Also, it improved the company’s ability to manage its supply chain effectively, reduce costs, and maintain product quality. By automating 
several business functions, Hershey also achieved greater consistency in its global operations, enabling the company to stay competitive and
agile in the dynamic consumer goods industry.

  c.2. implementation process & challenges
The implementation process of Hershey’s enterprise system involved several stages, including planning, designing, testing, and rollout. 
Hershey selected an integrated system that could manage its large-scale production, distribution, and sales activities across the globe. 
The company invested significant resources in training employees, reengineering business processes, and migrating data to the new system. 
However, the process came with challenges, including resistance from employees who were accustomed to legacy systems, the complexity of 
integrating diverse systems across departments, and the time constraints for transitioning without disrupting daily operations. 
The sheer size and scope of Hershey’s operations posed difficulties in ensuring smooth communication and coordination between teams.

  c.3. Key failure factors
Several factors contributed to Hershey’s struggles during the enterprise system implementation. One major issue was underestimating the 
complexity of the integration process. Hershey faced difficulties with aligning its supply chain and inventory management with the new system, 
which led to significant operational disruptions. The company also experienced delays in data migration and testing, resulting in errors in the 
production and delivery schedules. Moreover, there was a lack of effective change management, which caused resistance among employees who struggled 
to adapt to the new system. This combination of technical and human factors led to delays in achieving the full benefits of the system.

  c.4. outcomes & consequences
The outcomes of Hershey’s enterprise system implementation were mixed. In the short term, the company faced significant operational setbacks, 
including delays in delivering products to customers, which impacted sales during peak seasons. The company also incurred costs related to system errors, 
which further affected its financial performance. However, over time, Hershey was able to resolve many of the issues, improving its inventory management 
and supply chain operations. The company gained better visibility into its operations, allowing for more informed decision-making. Despite the initial setbacks, 
the long-term consequence of implementing the enterprise system was improved efficiency, reduced operational costs, and better alignment of business processes, 
helping Hershey remain competitive in the market.


